  "ExplanationCorrect": "This is an evaluative judgment requiring assessment of leverage reduction effectiveness, cost of each alternative, and residual financial flexibility. Alternative 3 provides the strongest structural improvement for several reasons. First, it achieves the largest absolute debt reduction ($140M, reducing total debt from $360M to $220M), bringing the D/E ratio from 2.40 to approximately 1.47 ($220M / $150M = 1.47), within the peer group range of 0.80–1.50. Second, the interest expense reduction at an assumed 8.0% average rate saves $11.2M annually, improving interest coverage from 1.39× ($40.0M / $28.8M) to an estimated 2.27× ($40.0M / $17.6M). Third, asset monetization avoids shareholder dilution entirely — a concern that Alternative 1 cannot address — while retaining 60% ownership in the midstream segment, preserving upside exposure. Fourth, under ASC 470-50, debt extinguishment through asset sale proceeds permanently reduces leverage rather than merely refinancing it. The sale of a minority interest (40%) rather than full divestiture preserves strategic optionality in the midstream business. Alternative 1 reduces leverage less (D/E to 1.68 vs 1.47), imposes 21% shareholder dilution, and leaves Sterling above the peer range. Alternative 2 does not reduce leverage at all and increases the interest burden, which credit rating agencies are unlikely to view favorably given the existing negative watch. Alternative 3 represents the analytically strongest path because it maximizes debt reduction, improves coverage ratios, and avoids both dilution and increased interest costs.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "Alternative 1 achieves meaningful leverage reduction (D/E from 2.40 to 1.68) but leaves Sterling above the peer group range of 0.80–1.50, meaning the credit rating agencies may not remove the negative watch. The 21% dilution is a material cost — existing shareholders lose roughly a fifth of their ownership stake. Furthermore, the interest savings calculation ($40M × 8.0% = $3.2M) assumes the retired debt is at the average rate; if the maturing notes carry a lower coupon, the savings would be less. Alternative 3 achieves greater debt reduction ($140M vs $40M), brings the D/E ratio within the peer range, and avoids dilution entirely.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "Alternative 2 does not address Sterling's fundamental leverage problem — it merely extends the maturity of $85M in notes at a higher interest rate. The credit rating agencies' negative watch was triggered by high leverage (D/E 2.40 vs industry 1.10), not by refinancing uncertainty. Completing a refinancing at a higher rate would likely confirm rather than resolve the agencies' concerns. The additional $1.28M in annual interest ($85M × 1.5%) further compresses the already-thin interest coverage ratio of 1.39×. Management that chooses to refinance at a higher rate without any deleveraging plan signals to creditors that it either cannot or will not reduce leverage.",
