    "C": "The operating income decline is the root cause, and it is cyclical, not structural. Mining demand typically recovers within 18–24 months, and Atlas's FY2025 operating income of $63M already exceeds interest expense of $35M by $28M. Ms. Vasquez should recommend negotiating a bridge loan to cover the $60M maturity until the cycle recovers, at which point Atlas can refinance from a stronger operating position.",
    "D": "Atlas's four-year TIE decline from 4.8× to 1.8× — a 62.5% deterioration — represents a structural impairment of debt service capacity, not merely cyclical weakness. Operating income could breach the 1.5× covenant with only a 16.7% further decline (from $63M to $52.5M, where $52.5M / $35M = 1.5×) — a plausible scenario given the 13.2% annual decline rate. The refinancing is at risk: at current credit quality, new lenders will likely demand significantly higher rates, tighter covenants, or collateral, and may not underwrite the full $60M. Ms. Vasquez should present a contingency plan that assumes partial or conditional refinancing."
