  "CognitiveLevel": "Analyze",
  "CommonTrapReference": "Trap: Excluding operating leases or omitting the preferred dividend tax gross-up from the fixed charge coverage ratio",
  "CorrectChoice": "C",
  "Difficulty": "Very Difficult",
  "DifficultyScore": 5,
  "ExplanationCorrect": "The correct fixed charge coverage ratio is 2.08×. Under ASC 842 and standard credit analysis methodology, the ratio is calculated as (EBIT + All Lease Payments) ÷ (Interest Expense + All Lease Payments + Preferred Dividends ÷ (1 − Tax Rate)). All lease obligations — both operating and capital — must be included because both represent contractual commitments requiring cash outflow before equity holders receive any return. Substituting Apex's data: ($5,000,000 + $800,000 + $500,000) ÷ ($1,200,000 + $800,000 + $500,000 + $400,000 ÷ 0.75) = $6,300,000 ÷ ($2,500,000 + $533,333) = $6,300,000 ÷ $3,033,333 = 2.08×. At 2.08×, Apex meets the 2.0× covenant with only 0.08× headroom — a margin so thin that any earnings decline would trigger a covenant breach. The $800,000 in operating lease payments are critical: excluding them would overstate the ratio. Preferred dividends must be grossed up to pre-tax for comparability.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "$2.46 results from omitting the $800,000 in operating lease payments from both numerator and denominator, treating only the proposed $500,000 capital lease as a fixed charge. Under ASC 842 and credit analysis standards, all lease obligations represent contractual commitments that must be satisfied before equity holders receive any return. Excluding operating leases understates the true fixed-charge burden and produces a misleadingly favorable ratio that does not reflect the economic substance of Apex's obligations.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "$2.52 results from omitting the tax-adjusted preferred dividends from the denominator. Because preferred dividends are paid from after-tax earnings, they must be grossed up by dividing by (1 − tax rate) to express them on a pre-tax basis comparable to interest and lease payments. At Apex's 25% tax rate, $400,000 in preferred dividends requires $533,333 of pre-tax earnings. Failing to gross up preferred dividends understates the total fixed-charge burden by $133,333 and produces an overstated ratio.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "$1.72 results from using EBIT alone in the numerator without adding back lease payments, and omitting the preferred dividend gross-up. The fixed charge coverage ratio adds lease payments to EBIT because leases are a financing cost already deducted in arriving at EBIT; failing to add them back double-counts the lease burden. A candidate reaching this answer both understates earnings available for fixed charges and misstates the total fixed-charge obligation.",
  "FormulaReference": "Fixed Charge Coverage = (EBIT + Lease Payments) / (Interest + Lease Payments + Preferred Dividends/(1-t))",
  "ItemStyle": "single-select",
  "LOSTag": "A.1",
  "Part": 2,
  "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
  "QuestionID": "P2-A-009",
