  "ExplanationCorrect": "The loan should be declined despite technical covenant compliance. Professional lending standards require analysts to evaluate both the quantitative ratios and the quality of the underlying financial data. Five qualitative factors collectively indicate that Northwind's reported ratios overstate its economic credit quality: (1) year-end inventory manipulation (window dressing) reduces the true current ratio; (2) 40% customer concentration with a six-month contract creates material revenue continuity risk; (3) $3.8 million in off-balance-sheet lease obligations would increase the true debt-to-equity ratio substantially; (4) $900,000 in accelerated revenue recognition reduces earnings quality; and (5) Northwind's 1.8 debt-to-equity ratio exceeds the 1.0 industry median by 80%. Taken individually, no single factor definitively warrants declining the loan, but the convergence of five distinct qualitative red flags creates an unacceptable risk profile. The credit analyst's professional obligation is to assess credit risk based on the substance of the borrower's financial condition, not merely its form.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "Treating quantitative covenant compliance as dispositive ignores the fundamental limitations of ratio analysis. Financial ratios can be manipulated through year-end transactions, are calculated using historical data that may not reflect future conditions, do not capture off-balance-sheet obligations, and are sensitive to accounting policy choices. Professional lending standards require evaluating the quality of reported financial data — not merely whether ratios exceed thresholds. Approving a loan based solely on covenant compliance when multiple qualitative indicators suggest the ratios overstate credit quality violates the principle that credit decisions must assess the borrower's capacity to repay.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "Monthly ratio reporting does not address the structural nature of the qualitative concerns. Year-end window dressing will recur at each reporting date; off-balance-sheet lease obligations will remain invisible in covenant calculations regardless of reporting frequency; and customer concentration risk with a six-month contract horizon cannot be mitigated by more frequent monitoring — the loss event, if it occurs, will be sudden and binary. More frequent reporting increases detection speed after deterioration occurs but does nothing to address the root causes that make the ratios unreliable indicators of credit quality.",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "Adjusting the interest rate to compensate for customer concentration risk addresses only one of five identified qualitative concerns. A 200-basis-point premium compensates for incremental expected loss but does not eliminate the fundamental credit quality issues. Risk-based pricing is appropriate for borrowers with genuine but quantifiable incremental risk — not for borrowers where multiple qualitative red flags collectively indicate that reported financial data may not faithfully represent the borrower's economic condition. Prudent underwriting requires declining credit when the information quality itself is in question.",
